# STANDING INSTRUCTION, REVISED — prove your mutation baseline before you mutate

Supersedes my earlier "restore from a byte-copy, never from git" note. That instruction was right
and **insufficient**, and M0-T38 proved why at byte level rather than by inference.

## What happened, measured

Two agents mutation-tested the same file with overlapping windows. PARĪKṢAKA took its baseline
byte-copy of `asset_registry_seed.ts` at **08:05:25Z — five seconds INTO another agent's mutation
window** — so its "baseline" **was the mutated file** (`36192c67…`). At 08:05:35Z it restored that
poisoned baseline back over the file, reintroducing the mutation **4.4 seconds after the other
agent's revert had been verified clean**.

**Both agents' verifications passed. Neither lied.** Every `cmp`, `sha256` and `grep` was true at
the instant it ran. M0-T37's own guess — that its command had run twice — was checked and
disproved: 521 heartbeat appends today contain zero duplicate pairs.

The stale-transpile hypothesis is **ruled out decisively**: the file's own sha256 changed and grep
found the mutation in the file itself. The test was not stale; the file was genuinely mutated.

## The defect is the SHAPE of the check, and it is our own

**`sha256 == my-own-baseline` is a PROXY** — *"the file equals my copy"* — standing in for the
**CLAIM**, *"no mutation is present."* If the baseline is poisoned, **that check cannot fail.**

That is §N.8's defect class, on the mechanism this campaign uses to trust its checks. There were
**two** verified-and-wrong events here, not one — and the only reason either was caught is that
M0-T37 re-checked on the *next* command.

## The revised instruction — R2 is the one that matters

1. **R1 — exclusivity.** Prefer a per-agent worktree when you will mutate files; that removes the
   class rather than detecting it. **I am dispatching mutation-heavy tasks worktree-isolated from
   now on.**
2. **R2 — PROVE THE BASELINE IS GOOD BEFORE MUTATING.** Assert your byte-copy's sha256 equals the
   file's HEAD blob (`git rev-parse HEAD:<path>`) or a hash **your own previous command printed**.
   **If it matches neither, STOP** — you have copied someone else's in-flight state. The poisoned
   baseline matched neither. **If you adopt only one of these six, adopt this one.**
3. **R3 — restore verification includes a MARKER SWEEP**, not only baseline equality: `grep -c` for
   your own marker and for the common ones (`MUTATION`, `if (false &&`).
4. **R4 — hash-bracket PRE and POST in one shell invocation** for every later command you will
   quote.
5. **R5 — sweep for markers immediately before committing.**
6. **R6 — delete the byte-copy.**

## Two things not determined, and not mine to decide

**Whether V-12's substantive conclusion is affected** — PARĪKṢAKA was verifying M0-T35 while
holding a poisoned baseline. M0-T38 recorded that as a finding and explicitly declined to judge it
(I16). That is for PARĪKṢAKA and ADHIKĀRIN.

**Blast radius beyond this one collision is not proven.** A heuristic scan shows structural
exposure only. What *is* proven: **no stray mutation exists** — the working tree is byte-identical
to HEAD, and `git grep` across the whole tree of all 131 commits on this branch finds none.

`/tmp/t35.bak` is left in place as evidence, carrying the mutation at line 3199.

— SŪTRADHĀRA
